A||||12||GT Bastard||Anybody running a street/strip chip??||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||03-16-2000||01:43 AM||||I think 1fst2m6 is...  I'm thinking about buying one for my GT.  If anyone has one, was there any noticeable performance gain?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Alex4mula||03-16-2000||08:32 AM||||About 8-9 years ago I bought what at that time was called the Hyperthech Stage 3 chip. It is to be used with a 160 deg thermostat and no cat. It said also that it disabled the EGR selenoid (i.e. no EGR gases) so it is suposed to be for Off road only. After using it for couple of years one day I put back the original chip and I did realy feel a drop in performance. Obviously I put the Hyper back and still is there. I don't know if the street/strip is the same.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||03-16-2000||08:58 AM||||Had one! Running a new custom chip. The ADS Strip chip is ok on the street. Will still pass emissions (unless you disconnect the EGR) you don't have to remove your cat. You'll only lose torque. You'll gain a sweeter sound but lose torque.. you pick. There was a measureable difference under the curve. No PEAK HP/Torque difference but between 1500 and 4500 there was a gain of 5hp and 6lbft(not at the same point on the curve). They change the ignition, and fuel mapping. The only difference in gas mileage is cause you'll drive the car harder than before due to the suggestion of more go. If you want to know if it's worth the money.  Yes it is... <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 03-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||88 Fiero||03-16-2000||11:08 AM||||When I bought my Formula... The previous owner have a custom chip made for the car. The Rev Limiter was eliminated and the car can rev all the way to 7000 RPM...without bogging down...||||reg||1
Z||000004||88 Fiero||03-16-2000||11:08 AM||||When I bought my Formula... The previous owner have a custom chip made for the car. The Rev Limiter was eliminated and the car can rev all the way to 7000 RPM...without bogging down...||||reg||1
Z||000005||88 Fiero||03-16-2000||11:08 AM||||When I bought my Formula... The previous owner have a custom chip made for the car. The Rev Limiter was eliminated and the car can rev all the way to 7000 RPM...without bogging down...||||reg||1
Z||000006||mwbackus||03-16-2000||12:59 PM||||According to my research and testing, all 88 V6 ECM's have the rev limiter disabled in code.  You can easily check to see if the PROM is stock by removing the access cover on the ECM and noting the part# on the chip.  My guess is the chip is stock and the previous owner was feeding you full of bull.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||03-17-2000||06:59 PM||||88 Fiero, It’s my understanding that the v6 fiero of all years redlines at 6000 rpm. If you disabled the rev-limiter and tried to exceed the engines redline in a stock engine; you would cause the engine to blow apart. Please correct me if I’m wrong.   <P>||||reg||3
Z||000009||GT Bastard||03-17-2000||07:09 PM||||I've known people to disable the rev limiter on their LT1's.  Some guys would rev them to 7000 rpm before shifting.... I've known one of them who had actually blown that motor doing so.  Others say they've done it regularly without problems.  I wouldn't try it myself....||||reg||1
Z||000010||88 Fiero||03-17-2000||07:23 PM||||88 Formula...my car was slightly built along with a cam..I've passed 6000 grand to 7 and never had a problem....my car is not an everyday commuter tho...but I dont always race it...||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||03-17-2000||08:01 PM||||88 Fiero, What would you have to do to the motor to achieve seven thousand rpm safely?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 03-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||1FST2M6||03-18-2000||06:26 PM||||My car pulls 7grand all day and has since June of 99. There is no gain in the additional rpm. The stock  motor runs out of go at about 5400... even shifting at 7 your still back at the sucking wind area in the HP and TQ numbers.. I've missed shifts on the DYNO and tached it to 7400 with no problems... The 60* V6 isn't as fragile as many think.. 6000 is a good shift point for a stock motor. 5400 on nitrous. keeps you in the biggest torque curve and she'll ALWAYS chirp any gearnitrous or not... I don't have a nice cam or anything else so i can't say what the higher RPMs would do for them but it's didn't help me. Just made me set the trigger in the ignition box to trip the Shift light at 6...  (just personal experiance...)  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Phaeton||03-19-2000||01:06 AM||||The stock crank rods and pistons are strong enough through 7000 in a well put together engine.  There are recomended oiling mods for the engines, due to design the rods get intermittant oil pressure and some simple machine work on the crank and main bearings dramaticly reduces the duration of no pressure.||||reg||6
